WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.270
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:10.490
In this paper, you will not only see an amazing
technique for two-way coupled fluid-solid

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.510
simulations, but you will also see some of
the most creative demonstrations of this new

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.460
method I’ve seen in a while.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.789
But first things first, what is this two way
coupling thing?

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:26.900
Two-way coupling in a fluid simulation means
being able to process contact.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.599
You see, the armadillos can collide with the
fluids, but the fluids are also allowed to

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.470
move the armadillos.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.800
And this new work can compute this kind of
contact.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.469
And by this I mean lots and lots of contact.

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:47.190
But one of the important lessons of this paper
is that we don’t necessarily need a scene

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.960
this crazy to be dominated by two-way coupling.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.940
Have a look at this experiment with these
adorable duckies, and below, the propellers

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:02.500
are starting up, please don’t be one of
those graphics papers.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.000
Okay..okay, good.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.250
We dodged this one.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:10.940
So the propellers are not here to dismember
things, they are here to spin up to 160 rpm,

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:17.460
and since they are two-way coupled, they pump
water from one tank to the other, raising

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.440
the water levels, allowing the duckies to
pass.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.820
An excellent demonstration of a proper algorithm
that can compute two-way coupling really well.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.940
And simulating this scene is much, much more
challenging than we might think.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.340
Why is that?

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:39.210
Note that the speed of the propellers is quite
high, which is a huge challenge to previous

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.270
methods.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.581
If we wish to complete the simulation in a
reasonable amount of time, it simulates the

00:01:44.581 --> 00:01:51.640
interaction incorrectly and no ducks can pass.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.230
The new technique can simulate this correctly,
and not only that, but it is also 4.5 times

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.060
faster than the previous method.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.640
Also, check out this elegant demonstration
of two-way coupling.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:11.000
We start slowly unscrewing this bolt…and…nothing
too crazy going on here.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.430
However, look!

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:18.980
We have tiny cutouts in the bolt, allowing
the water to start gushing out.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:27.050
The pipe was made transparent so we can track
the water levels slowly decreasing, and finally,

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:32.330
when the bolt falls out, we get some more
two-way coupling action with the water.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.490
Once more, such a beautiful demonstration
of a difficult to simulate phenomenon.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.490
Loving it.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:45.720
A traditional technique cannot simulate this
properly, unless we add a lot of extra computation.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.540
At which point, it is still unstable… ouch!

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:55.410
And with even more extra computation, we can
finally do this, but hold on to your papers,

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:01.260
because the new proposed technique can do
it about 10 times faster.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.530
It also supports contact against rich geometry
as well.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.370
Look, we have a great deal going on here.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:13.770
You are seeing up to 38 million fluid particles
interacting with these walls given with lots

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:20.310
of rich geometry, and there will be interaction
with mud, and elastic trees as well.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.730
This can really do them all.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:27.730
And did you notice that throughout this video,
we saw a lot of delta t-s.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.940
What are those?

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.360
Delta t is something that we call time step
size.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.630
The smaller this number is, the tinier the
time steps with which we can advance the simulation

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:44.209
when computing every interaction, and hence,
the more steps there are to compute.

00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:49.490
In simpler words, generally, time step size
is an important factor in the computation

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:55.340
time, and the smaller this is, the slower,
but more accurate the simulation will be.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:01.160
This is why we needed to reduce the time steps
by more than 30 times to get a stable simulation

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.520
here with the previous method.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.110
And this paper proposes a technique that can
get away with time steps that are typically

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:13.580
from 10 times to a 100 times larger than previous
methods.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.910
And it is still stable.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.180
That is an incredible achievement.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.000
So what does that mean in a practical case?

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.150
Well, hold on to your papers, because this
means that it is up to 58 times faster than

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:28.150
previous methods.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:29.150
58 times!

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.150
Whoa.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:36.509
With a previous method, I would need to run
something for nearly two months, and the new

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:40.650
method would be able to compute the same within
a day.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.080
Witchcraft, I’m telling you.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.520
What a time to be alive!

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:50.449
Also, as usual, I couldn’t resist creating
a slow-motion version of some of these videos,

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:54.810
so if this is something that you wish to see,
make sure to visit our Instagram page in the

00:04:54.810 --> 00:05:44.180
video description 

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.819
for more.

00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:50.269
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

